734375, 0.01507568359375, -0.00832366943359375, -0.022186279296875, -0.01529693603515625, 0.00833892822265625, -0.0215301513671875, 0.00574493408203125, 0.0452880859375, 0.033233642578125, 0.006351470947265625, 0.01186370849609375, 0.00537872314453125, 0.0108642578125, 0.036590576171875, -0.028564453125, -0.025665283203125, 0.030029296875, 0.012481689453125, 0.0133056640625, -0.01287841796875, 0.00986480712890625, -0.020843505859375, -0.0090179443359375, 0.03314208984375, -0.006061553955078125, -0.0005984306335449219, 0.02410888671875, -0.01094818115234375, 0.0286712646484375, -0.0028400421142578125, 0.0015888214111328125, -0.0023956298828125, 0.017486572265625, 0.0155487060546875, 0.0240478515625, -0.0032367706298828125, 0.0188446044921875, 0.024810791015625, -0.00443267822265625, 0.031768798828125, -0.01511383056640625, 0.0153656005859375, 0.0220184326171875, -0.047088623046875, 0.012359619140625, -0.0229949951171875, 0.020782470703125, 0.022613525390625, -0.032470703125, 0.0123443603515625, 0.01233673095703125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "District heating networks are essential for achieving low-carbon heat supplies, but their efficient operation is increasingly challenged by the integration of heterogeneous, time-varying renewable sources, thermal storage, and complex meshed (cyclic) topologies. In such cyclic networks with multiple suppliers and storage units, operational optimization is complicated by nonlinear hydraulic and thermal interactions, inter-temporal coupling via storage, and the combinatorial complexity of flow directions in cycles. Existing methods typically address only acyclic or linearized models, or cannot efficiently guarantee global optimality for realistic, multi-period, cyclic networks with nonlinearities and storage. For example, in a network with two suppliers and a storage tank connected in a loop, determining the optimal flow and temperature profiles over time requires resolving nonlinear pressure drops (which relate flow to pressure loss in pipes), mixing of water at network junctions (which determines outgoing temperatures), and storage dynamics (which couple decisions across time), all while avoiding non-physical cyclic flows\u2014flows that circulate endlessly in a loop without serving any demand. Standard linear or tree-based approaches do not address these challenges. To our knowledge, no prior work provides a global optimization framework for nonlinear, cyclic district heating networks with multiple suppliers and storage, along with scalable algorithmic enhancements and comprehensive evaluation on realistic benchmarks. This work fills that gap.\n\n---\n\n**Physical Model and Key Assumptions:**  \nWe model the district heating network as a directed graph \\( G = (V, E) \\), where nodes \\( V \\) represent junctions, suppliers, consumers, or storage units, and edges \\( E \\) represent pipes. The following physical assumptions are made for tractability and clarity:\n- **Perfect Mixing:** Water at each node is assumed to mix instantaneously and perfectly, so outgoing temperature is a flow-weighted average of incoming streams.\n- **Neglect of Heat Losses:** Heat losses along pipes are neglected; all pipes are assumed perfectly insulated. This simplifies the thermal model and is justified for short time horizons or well-insulated networks. Extensions to include heat losses are possible but not considered here.\n- **Constant Fluid Properties:** Water density and specific heat are assumed constant and independent of temperature and pressure, which is standard for moderate temperature ranges in district heating.\n- **No Minimum Flow Thresholds:** Minimum flow rates in pipes are neglected, assuming that operational flows always exceed practical thresholds.\n- **No Pipe Capacity Limits:** Unless otherwise stated, pipe flow rates are assumed unbounded for modeling simplicity; practical limits can be added as linear constraints.\n- **No Pressure-Driven Demand:** Consumer demands are assumed fixed and independent of network pressure.\n\nThese assumptions are standard in the literature for first-principles modeling and allow focus on the core nonlinear and combinatorial challenges. Where relevant, omitted constraints (e.g., pipe capacities, heat losses) are discussed in the limitations section.\n\n---\n\n**Main Contributions:**  \n- **Global Optimization Framework:** We develop a global optimization framework for cyclic district heating networks with multiple suppliers and storage, capturing nonlinear hydraulic and thermal constraints under the above physical assumptions.\n- **Explicit Problem Formulation:** We provide a summary of the full nonlinear mixed-integer optimization problem, including variables, objective, and main constraints, with intuitive explanations of their physical meaning.\n- **Algorithmic Enhancements:** We propose five novel algorithmic enhancements to accelerate spatial branch-and-bound for these networks, each with explicit mathematical or algorithmic formulation and illustrative examples. Key steps and decision criteria are summarized below; full pseudocode and technical details are provided in the supplementary material.\n- **Comprehensive Evaluation:** We provide a detailed evaluation on both generated and real-world benchmark instances, with explicit parameter ranges, statistical analysis, and confidence intervals for key metrics. Main evaluation metrics and findings are summarized below.\n- **Time Decomposition Analysis:** We analyze a time decomposition approach for multi-period operation with storage, rigorously discussing its assumptions, empirical validity, and limitations.\n- **Discussion and Future Work:** We discuss limitations in detail, provide concrete next steps, and outline plans for open-sourcing code and data, including current availability.\n\n---\n\n**Mathematical Formulation:**  \n**Variables and Notation:**  \n- \\( q_{e,t} \\): Flow rate (mass per unit time) in pipe \\( e \\) at time \\( t \\). Positive or negative values indicate direction.\n- \\( T_{v,t} \\): Water temperature at node \\( v \\) at time \\( t \\).\n- \\( s_{j,t} \\): State of charge (energy stored) for storage unit \\( j \\) at time \\( t \\).\n- \\( x_{e,t} \\): Binary variable indicating flow direction in pipe \\( e \\) at time \\( t \\) (1 if forward, 0 if reverse).\n- \\( q_{j,t}^{\\text{charge}} \\), \\( q_{j,t}^{\\text{discharge}} \\): Non-negative variables representing the amount of energy charged into or discharged from storage unit \\( j \\) at time \\( t \\).\n- \\( \\Delta p_{e,t} \\): Pressure drop across pipe \\( e \\) at time \\( t \\).\n- \\( k_e \\): Pipe friction coefficient for pipe \\( e \\).\n- \\( \\eta_{\\text{charge}} \\), \\( \\eta_{\\text{discharge}} \\): Charging/discharging efficiency for storage units.\n- \\( T_{i,t}^{\\min} \\), \\( T_{i,t}^{\\max} \\): Minimum and maximum allowable temperatures for supplier \\( i \\).\n- \\( S_j^{\\max} \\): Maximum storage capacity for storage unit \\( j \\).\n\n**Objective:**  \nMinimize total operational cost over all time periods, including supplier fuel costs and storage operation costs:\n\\[\n\\min \\sum_{t} \\sum_{i \\in \\text{suppliers}} c_i(q_{i,t}, T_{i,t}) + \\sum_{j \\in \\text{storage}} c_j(s_{j,t})\n\\]\nwhere \\( c_i \\) and \\( c_j \\) are cost functions for suppliers and storage, respectively.\n\n**Main Constraints (with physical interpretation):**  \n- **Nonlinear pressure drops:**  \n  \\[\n  \\Delta p_{e,t} = k_e \\cdot q_{e,t}^2 \\quad \\forall e, t\n  \\]\n  (Pressure loss in each pipe is proportional to the square of the flow, modeling pipe friction.)\n- **Temperature mixing at nodes:**  \n  \\[\n  T_{v,t}^{\\text{out}} = \\frac{\\sum_{e \\in \\text{in}(v)} q_{e,t} T_{e,t}^{\\text{in}}}{\\sum_{e \\in \\text{in}(v)} q_{e,t}} \\quad \\forall v, t\n  \\]\n  (Outgoing temperature at a node is the flow-weighted average of incoming temperatures, assuming perfect mixing.)\n- **Supply/return temperature bounds:**  \n  \\[\n  T_{i,t}^{\\min} \\leq T_{i,t} \\leq T_{i,t}^{\\max} \\quad \\forall i \\in \\text{suppliers}, t\n  \\]\n  (Ensures water temperatures remain within safe and efficient operating ranges.)\n- **Mass and energy conservation:**  \n  \\[\n  \\sum_{e \\in \\text{in}(v)} q_{e,t} = \\sum_{e \\in \\text{out}(v)} q_{e,t} \\quad \\forall v, t\n  \\]\n  (Total inflow equals total outflow at each node, ensuring no mass accumulation.)\n- **Thermal storage dynamics:**  \n  \\[\n  s_{j,t+1} = s_{j,t} + \\eta_{\\text{charge}} q_{j,t}^{\\text{charge}} - \\eta_{\\text{discharge}} q_{j,t}^{\\text{discharge}} \\quad \\forall j, t\n  \\]\n  (Storage state evolves based on charged/discharged energy, accounting for efficiency losses.)\n- **Cycle-breaking constraints:**  \n  For each fundamental cycle \\( C \\) in the network and each time \\( t \\), we enforce:\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{e \\in C} x_{e,t} \\leq |C| - 1\n  \\]\n  This constraint is both necessary and sufficient to prevent non-physical cyclic flows, i.e., flows that circulate endlessly in a loop without serving any demand. If all edges in a cycle have flow in the same direction, a closed loop of flow is created with no net supply or demand, which is not physically meaningful. By ensuring that at least one edge in each cycle does not participate in the same direction, the formation of such non-physical cycles is prevented. This approach is standard in network flow optimization (see, e.g., [1] for similar cycle elimination in power and gas networks).\n- **Variable domains:**  \n  \\( q_{e,t} \\in \\mathbb{R} \\), \\( T_{v,t} \\in [T^{\\min}, T^{\\max}] \\), \\( s_{j,t} \\in [0, S_j^{\\max}] \\), \\( x_{e,t} \\in \\{0,1\\} \\), \\( q_{j,t}^{\\text{charge}}, q_{j,t}^{\\text{discharge}} \\geq 0 \\)\n\nA full list of constraints and their mathematical forms is provided in the supplementary material. For clarity, a table of symbols is also included in the supplementary material.\n\n---\n\n**Algorithmic Enhancements:**  \n\n1. **Cycle-Breaking Constraints:**  \n   As described above, for each fundamental cycle \\( C \\), the constraint\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{e \\in C} x_{e,t} \\leq |C| - 1\n   \\]\n   is added at each time \\( t \\) to prevent non-physical cyclic flows."}}